#: src/mathlib.c:822
msgid "Mathlib Help"
msgstr "Mathlib-hulp"

#: src/mathlib.c:858
msgid "Mathlib Benchmark Help"
msgstr "Mathlib-maatstaafhulp"

#: src/mathlib.c:860
msgid ""
"Jobs are forked and run in parallel by xnec2c, whereas threads are used by "
"the mathlib implementation to parallelize the linear algebra calculation of "
"one job.  Each job receives an equal share of the processors as its thread "
"count, and the --threads N option states a count of its own instead.  An "
"exported thread-count variable states the count itself and overrides both; "
"xnec2c names that variable at startup and refuses --threads while one is "
"set.\n"
"\n"
"Each benchmark specifies a complexity to indicate approximately how long it "
"will take to run.  For example, O(N) means the time will scale with N and "
"O(N*J) means it will scale with N*J:\n"
"  * N is the number of mathlibs selected here:\n"
"      File->Mathlib Benchmarks->Selected Mathlibs\n"
"  * J is the number of jobs specified on the command line with -j (eg, -j "
"8)\n"
"  * T is the number of threads one job gives the linear algebra library\n"
"\n"
"Higher complexity options run more benchmarks to provide additional "
"information about how you might optimize your xnec2c runtime.  The benchmark "
"menu options are as follows:  \n"
"\n"
"* Parallel (-j N): O(N/J) time: Benchmark each mathlib in parallel with the "
"number of jobs specified on the command line with -j.\n"
"\n"
"* Single Job (-j 1): O(N) time: Benchmark each mathlib with a single job "
"even if -j was specified on the command line. This is useful to find the "
"fastest interactive mathlib that is used when the frequency is changed in "
"the UI by changing a Frequency MHz +/- widget or (right-)clicking on the "
"frequency plot\n"
"\n"
"* Iterate -j N/=2: O(N*log2(J)) time: Benchmark each mathlib in parallel "
"initially with the number of jobs specified on the command line with -j, and "
"then reduce the number jobs by half for each subsequent iteration until only "
"one job remains.  This is useful to find a balance between the number of "
"parallel jobs specified by -j and the number of threads used by the linear "
"algebra library as modified by the environment variables listed above.\n"
"\n"
"* Iterate -j N-=2: O(N*J) time: Same as -j N/=2 above, but instead decrement "
"the number of jobs by 1.  This takes the longest to run.\n"
"\n"
"* Threads (-j 1, --threads N/=2): O(N*log2(T)) time: Benchmark each mathlib "
"with a single job, starting with every processor given to the linear algebra "
"library and halving the thread count for each subsequent iteration until one "
"thread remains.  This finds the thread count beyond which one matrix solve "
"stops gaining, which is the count that matters when a sweep has fewer "
"frequencies left to compute than you have processors.\n"
msgstr ""
"Take word deur xnec2c gefork en parallel uitgevoer, terwyl drade deur die "
"mathlib-implementering gebruik word om die lineêre-algebraberekening van een "
"taak te paralleliseer. Elke taak ontvang 'n gelyke deel van die verwerkers "
"as sy draadtelling, en die --threads N-opsie stel eerder sy eie telling. 'n "
"Uitgevoerde draadtellingveranderlike stel die telling self en oorskryf "
"albei; xnec2c noem daardie veranderlike by opstart en weier --threads terwyl "
"een gestel is.\n"
"\n"
"Elke maatstaf spesifiseer 'n kompleksiteit om by benadering aan te dui hoe "
"lank dit sal neem om uit te voer. Byvoorbeeld beteken O(N) dat die tyd met N "
"sal skaal en O(N*J) dat dit met N*J sal skaal:\n"
"  * N is die aantal mathlibs wat hier gekies is:\n"
"      File->Mathlib Benchmarks->Selected Mathlibs\n"
"  * J is die aantal take wat op die opdragreël met -j gespesifiseer is (bv. -"
"j 8)\n"
"  * T is die aantal drade wat een taak aan die lineêre-algebrabiblioteek "
"gee\n"
"\n"
"Opsies met hoër kompleksiteit voer meer maatstawwe uit om bykomende "
"inligting te verskaf oor hoe jy jou xnec2c-looptyd kan optimaliseer. Die "
"maatstafkieslysopsies is soos volg:  \n"
"\n"
"* Parallel (-j N): O(N/J) tyd: Meet elke mathlib parallel met die aantal "
"take wat op die opdragreël met -j gespesifiseer is.\n"
"\n"
"* Enkele taak (-j 1): O(N) tyd: Meet elke mathlib met 'n enkele taak, selfs "
"al is -j op die opdragreël gespesifiseer. Dit is nuttig om die vinnigste "
"interaktiewe mathlib te vind wat gebruik word wanneer die frekwensie in die "
"gebruikerskoppelvlak verander word deur 'n Frekwensie MHz +/- legstuk te "
"verander of deur op die frekwensiegrafiek te (regs)klik\n"
"\n"
"* Herhaal -j N/=2: O(N*log2(J)) tyd: Meet elke mathlib aanvanklik parallel "
"met die aantal take wat op die opdragreël met -j gespesifiseer is, en "
"verminder dan die aantal take met die helfte vir elke daaropvolgende "
"herhaling totdat slegs een taak oorbly. Dit is nuttig om 'n balans te vind "
"tussen die aantal parallelle take wat deur -j gespesifiseer word en die "
"aantal drade wat deur die lineêre-algebrabiblioteek gebruik word soos deur "
"die omgewingsveranderlikes hierbo gewysig.\n"
"\n"
"* Herhaal -j N-=2: O(N*J) tyd: Dieselfde as -j N/=2 hierbo, maar verminder "
"eerder die aantal take met 1. Dit neem die langste om uit te voer.\n"
"\n"
"* Drade (-j 1, --threads N/=2): O(N*log2(T)) tyd: Meet elke mathlib met 'n "
"enkele taak, beginnende met elke verwerker aan die lineêre-algebrabiblioteek "
"gegee en halveer die draadtelling vir elke daaropvolgende herhaling totdat "
"een draad oorbly. Dit vind die draadtelling waarbo een matriksoplossing nie "
"meer aanwins lewer nie, wat die telling is wat saak maak wanneer 'n sweep "
"minder frekwensies oor het om te bereken as wat jy verwerkers het.\n"

